  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 AM MST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nThe weather patterns often remind may of my college work in \nmeteorology many moons ago. Some of the courses, like map analysis, \nsynoptic and mesoscale (the forecasting classes) as well as severe \nweather, were the exciting and interesting classes. Then we had \ncourses like dynamics (I still have bad flashbacks about having to \nderive the equations almost 30 years later) that are, no offense to \nmy former professors, rather boring and tedious. The forecast over \nthe next five days or so, falls into the latter, rather boring.\n\nThings today look fairly similar to yesterday with a flat ridging / \nzonal flow over the area. This means temperatures fairly similar to \nyesterday (8 to 12 degrees), cloud cover similar to yesterday \n(passing areas of mid to high clouds) and (noticing a pattern), wind \nfairly similar to yesterday (light to moderate for most, but gusty \nin westerly flow locations, like the lee of the Absarokas and \nCasper). Humidity does look a bit higher than yesterday though, so \nfire concerns should be a bit less (there had to be one difference).\n\nThe one bit of rather measured excitement will occur late tonight \ninto tomorrow as a weak shortwave brushes by to the north. There \nwill be two main impacts. One will be the chance, although a small \none for some showers across northern Wyoming. The chance outside of \nthe models is very small through, generally less than 1 out of 5 \nwith any amounts rather negligible. But even in the mountains, the \nchance of greater than a tenth of an inch of precipitation is less \nthan 1 out of 10. All showers should be east of the area by mid \nafternoon. The other impact will be for gusty wind across the area. \nThe 700 millibar winds only max out at around 30 to 35 knots, so \nhigh wind is not expected. However, much of the area has at least a \n3 out of 5 chance of wind gusts on Tuesday, with the strongest gusts \nin the afternoon with wave passage and period of maximum diurnal \nmixing. Wind should decrease at night as the wave moves away and the \natmosphere decouples.\n\nWe then enter a boring period as strong ridging moves over the area \nstarting Wednesday and lasting at least into Friday. This means a \nfew pleasant days with sunshine and mild temperatures. Wind should \nbe light through most of the period for most locations. The \nexception will be in the favored southwest flow locations, which may \nsee a gusty wind as the pressure gradient tightens ahead of the next \npotential weather maker.\n\nAnd notice the word potential (aren't modifiers wonderful). Because, \nas would be expected this far out, there is a fair amount of \nuncertainty with the details. Different ensemble members are all \nover the place, as well as the deterministic models. All guidance \nhas the ridge breaking down, although at different speeds. For \nexample, the European is faster and shows the Pacific system being a \nlittle stronger, with the associated trough becoming negatively \ntilted and bring a bit more wrap around moisture to northern \nWyoming. The GFS is much slower, holding the ridge and not \nbringing the system in until Sunday night or Monday. The trough\nis also much weaker and having more of a west / southwesterly \nflow upslope - downslope pattern with the precipitation, with \nmost falling in the western mountains. It also means a longer \nperiod of above normal temperatures and gusty southwest wind; \nwith potentially elevated fire weather to portions of central \nWyoming. The European does tend to be too fast with Pacific \nsystems this far out, so the greater chance (about a 2 out of 3 \nchance) is for a slower arrival of the precipitation. Stay tuned\nas we get closer to next weekend as the details come into \nbetter focus.",
